    <div class="prose max-w-none">
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">In B2B, email is where the real work happens: quotes, submittals, purchase orders and the question a contractor sends at 6 a.m. That also makes it one of the best marketing channels a manufacturer has, as long as the emails are useful.</p>
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl mb-4">Why email still works in industrial sales</h2>
      <ul class="checklist mb-8">
        <li><span>Buying decisions take weeks and involve several people: engineers, purchasing, the branch manager, the installer.</span></li>
        <li><span>Your distributors' counter staff can only sell what they remember. Email keeps your products in front of them.</span></li>
        <li><span>You own the list. No algorithm decides who sees your product update.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl mb-4">Who to email</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">One newsletter for everyone is the most common mistake. Split your list into the groups that need different information:</p>
      <ul class="checklist mb-8">
        <li><span><strong>Distributors and dealers:</strong> branch managers and counter staff who recommend products every day.</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>Contractors and installers:</strong> the people who choose what gets installed and call when something doesn't fit.</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>Engineers and specifiers:</strong> they need data, drawings and submittal documents, not promotions.</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>Existing customers by product line:</strong> so a pool supply customer never gets the HVAC update.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl mb-4">What to send</h2>
      <ul class="checklist mb-8">
        <li><span>New products and updated spec sheets, with the PDF one click away.</span></li>
        <li><span>Discontinued parts and their replacements, lead time changes and price updates before they take effect.</span></li>
        <li><span>Installation tips: a short video or a one-page quick reference that saves a call to your support line.</span></li>
        <li><span>Counter staff training: how to explain a product in 30 seconds and which questions to ask the customer.</span></li>
        <li><span>Trade show invitations and new catalog releases.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl mb-4">The email that sells the most: the quote follow-up</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">Most quotes die because nobody follows up. A simple sequence, sent from the sales rep and not from a marketing address, recovers many of them:</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8 space-y-3">
        <li>Same day: thanks for the request, the quote and the spec sheets for the items quoted.</li>
        <li>Day 5: answer the question buyers usually ask about that product, or share an installation photo.</li>
        <li>Day 14: a short check-in with current lead time and a direct phone number.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">This sequence can run automatically and still sound personal. It is the kind of follow-up we set up in our <a href="/en/solutions/ai-automation/" style="color:var(--primary)">bilingual AI automation</a>.</p>
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl mb-4">Keep it simple</h2>
      <ul class="checklist mb-8">
        <li><span>One useful email a month beats four that nobody reads.</span></li>
        <li><span>Plain layout that reads well on a phone at a job site. One clear call to action.</span></li>
        <li><span>Send it from a real person with a reply-to that someone answers.</span></li>
        <li><span>Measure replies, downloads and quote requests. Open rates are unreliable since many email apps load images automatically.</span></li>
        <li><span>Include your physical address and a working unsubscribe link, as US law (CAN-SPAM) requires for commercial email.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl mb-4">Send it in Spanish where it counts</h2>
      <p class="text-gray-500 leading-relaxed mb-8">If your installers, warehouse crews or counter staff work in Spanish, a Spanish version of the update gets read and forwarded. Have technical terms reviewed by a native speaker; a literal translation of a product name can confuse more than it helps. See our <a href="/en/solutions/technical-translation/" style="color:var(--primary)">technical translation</a> service.</p>
